    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d152">

                <pb facs="0336" n="306" xml:id="pg306"/>

                <head>152. Circular Telegram From the Department of State
                    to Certain Diplomatic Missions<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d152-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1960-09-03"><hi rendition="#italic">September 3,
                        1960</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>345. Circular 341.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d152-f2"/> While desiring avoid Soviets’
                    turning UNGA into worldwide “summit,”
                    Department does not wish put pressure on leaders African states, especially new
                    states, not to attend, so long as this is not result Soviet pressure. Posts in
                    dealing with reftel should avoid giving such
                    impression and should indicate African chiefs of state, prime ministers, foreign
                    ministers and others will be most welcome in US, although they may wish weigh
                    benefits obtained by attendance on occasion admission their country to UN against possibility such attendance might be used
                    by Soviets turn session into something highly undesirable from their, as well as
                    our, point of view.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Herter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d152-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d152-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 320/9–360. Confidential; Priority.
                    Drafted by Green and Ferguson; cleared
                    by AFN and in substance by Kohler; and approved by Penfield who signed for Herter. Sent to 17
                    posts in Africa. The time of transmission is not legible on the source text.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d152-f2" n="2" xml:id="d152-f2-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d151-f1">footnote 1</ref>, <hi rendition="#italic"><ref
                            target="#d152">supra</ref></hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
